A||N||3||Babygirl||Windshield wiper motor||||5||
Z||000000||Babygirl||10-11-2000||10:34 PM||||Yesterday I was driving my '84 SE down I-205 when my windshield wipers suddenly stopped working.  About 30 seconds later, they came on again.  Then they went out, and the same thing happened.  In Oregon, this is not a good thing.  It rains all the time here.  What's the easiest way to fix and/or replace the motor?  Do I HAVE to rip the dash board completely out??<P>------------------<BR>~Darcy & Laura<BR>1984 Black SE Fiero||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||10-11-2000||10:39 PM||||So you <B>really</B> have intermittent wipers, eh  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">?<P>I am pretty certain that changing the wiper motor is fairly easy.  Never had to do it myself, so I'm only guessing - but I believe all of the linkages are accessible by removing the grill from the cowl in front of the windshield.        ||||reg||12
Z||000002||Oreif||10-12-2000||02:07 AM||||Check the left side (driver side) of the kick panel. There are 2 large connectors. One is for the door locks, windows, mirrors, etc and one a little higher up is for engine controls and such. If it is loose damaged or cracked you could have intermittent wipers and intermittent or non-working gauges. ||||reg||1
Z||000003||EdsB52||10-12-2000||03:28 AM||||If you have to change the motor, you'll find it just in front of the windshield on the drivers side. You have to open the hood, pull up that black plastic webbing at the base of the windshield, and look inside the access hole in front of the wiper arm (l/h). It's a pain in the ass to access and remove, so as a previous poster stated, I would look at other things first. They rarely go bad, so check it out.||||reg||1
